            <div xml:id="d443">

                <pb facs="0458" n="454" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg454"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20 Manchuria/31: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Salisbury</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-07-05T15:00:00+08:00">July 5, 1938—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:56 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>417. Embassy’s 411, July 2, 4 p.m.<anchor n="8" xml:id="d443-f1"/> Following from
                    Mukden.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“47, July 2, 11 a.m.</p>
                    <p>Your June 29, 4 p.m. I am reliably informed that Ma Chan Shan’s command has
                        made contact with revolted troops and there is fighting <pb facs="0459"
                            n="455" xml:id="pg455"/> going on in southwest Jehol with Chinese in
                        control of some localities, Police detachments from Mukden are reported to
                        have been sent south evidently to help maintain order behind disturbed
                        zone.”</p>
                </quote>
                <p>Repeated to Embassy, Hankow, Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
